      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait, if that's right then why am i still getting access denied? i tried adding /* to the end of the resource line like \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\" and it still failed. \n\nis there some other way this can be blocked? like maybe something at the account level or a service control policy (scp)? i feel like i'm being locked out of my own stuff for no reason.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Since \"Access Denied\" persists despite correct IAM settings, the most likely culprit is your AWS account-level Service Control Policy (SCP) or Terraforming configuration which can override bucket policies. An SCP is the highest layer of permissions in AWS; if a service-level SCP denies `s3:PutObject` globally for your account, no Lambda execution role, even one with perfectly formatted policies, can execute that action. However, most users find the real issue lies earlier in the chain: specifically, whether your Lambda's trust policy explicitly whitelists `s3.amazonaws.com` (or the region-specific hostname like `s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com`) alongside the necessary permissions. While SCPs prevent access entirely by design, it is highly probable that an incorrect resource string in your Lambda's inline policy is silently blocking the specific object you are trying to write, rather than just the bucket itself. To prove this isn't SCP on its own, try creating a simple Lambda function (that you have full access to executing and viewing logs for) to test if the same permission configuration fails there first.",
